
=== EXPERIMENT 1: DOES BREAKOUT'S SYNTHESIS MAKE PONG CHEAPER? (100 seeds) ===

  Same worker, same verifiers. Only difference: whether Pong can read the packs Breakout produced.

  Mean attempts to pass the verifier (95% CI). Chance = position of the first correct candidate.

  family      breakout(cold)   pong(cold control)   pong(warm, from breakout packs)
  movement    1.93 ±0.17       1.95 ±0.15           1.00 ±0.00
  wall        1.73 ±0.15       1.68 ±0.15           1.00 ±0.00
  paddle      2.39 ±0.22       2.45 ±0.21           1.00 ±0.00
  round       2.47 ±0.22       2.42 ±0.21           1.00 ±0.00
  brick       2.53 ±0.22       —                    —  (breakout-only)
  ai          —                2.59 ±0.21           2.30 ±0.22  (pong-only: no prior pack exists)

  total attempts/build   breakout 11.05 ±0.42 | pong cold 11.09 ±0.41 | pong warm 6.30 ±0.22
  principal interruptions/build (cumulative-risk gates, excl. ship): pong cold 0.01 ±0.02 | pong warm 0.00 ±0.00

  PASS  L1 every build of every condition ships (passes its playtest through the ship gate)  — 300/300 shipped
  PASS  L2 the tasks are genuinely hard cold (shared families avg > 1.8 attempts)  — cold shared mean 2.13
  PASS  L3 warm Pong solves every shared family on the first try  — movement 1.00, wall 1.00, paddle 1.00, round 1.00
  PASS  L4 the reduction is statistically decisive (z > 10)  — cold 2.13 → warm 1.00, z = 22.9
  PASS  L5 transfer is specific: the family with no prior pack shows no significant discount (|z| < 3)  — ai cold 2.59 vs warm 2.30, z = 1.9 (shared families: z = 22.9)
  PASS  L6 whole-build cost drops  — 11.1 → 6.3 attempts per build
  PASS  L8 every retry after a delta carried a replan, and the engine refused none  — 1344 retries, 1344 replans, 0 refusals across 300 builds
  PASS  L7 promotion is never automatic: lessons verified in two games are candidates, not Knowledge; single-game lessons are plain Task  — shared: task+candidate,task+candidate,task+candidate,task+candidate | single-game: ai=task,brick=task

  INFO  principal interruptions were negligible in both conditions (0.01 vs 0.00 per build), so no governance-load claim is made.

